What is the rationale for automatically selling items that are
dropped accidentally in shops?  When you voluntarily drop an item,
you don't have to choose between dropping it and selling it; the
shopkeeper is just fine with items he doesn't own being on the floor. 
And yet when it's involuntary, you get situations like this:

A tin for 6 zorkmids.  Pay? [yn] (n) 
You bought a tin for 6 gold pieces.  "Thank you for shopping in
Matagami's general store!"
What do you want to eat? [kmxADFHLU or ?*] 
It is not so easy to open this tin.  You succeed in opening the tin.
It smells like elves.
Eat it? [yn] (n) 
You consume french fried elf.  Eating deep fried food made your
fingers very slippery.
Your weapon slips from your hands.
You relinquish an uncursed battle-axe named Cleaver and receive 187
gold pieces in compensation.
# chat
an uncursed battle-axe named Cleaver, price 8000 zorkmids, finest
quality.

Does it date back to a time when all items on the floor in a shop
belonged to the shopkeeper, or is it a deliberate annoyance?  Sure, I
should add "don't eat tins in shops" to a long list of other
don't-do-that things, but it just strikes me as odd that the
shopkeeper can't ask you whether you want to sell as he would with a
normal drop.

And, of course, the pitiful sum Mr. Matagami gave me for it compared
to his asking price just adds insult to injury.  Unfortunately, my
attempt at retribution didn't go nearly as well as it did in several
wizmode trials using a similar character. :-P

